def spiral_order(grid):
    if not grid or not grid[0]:
        return []
    h = len(grid)
    w = len(grid[0])
    result = []
    for layer in range(min(h, w) // 2):
        for x in range(layer, w - layer):
            result.append(grid[layer][x])
        for y in range(layer + 1, h - layer):
            result.append(grid[y][w - layer - 1])
        for x in reversed(range(layer, w - layer)):
            result.append(grid[h - layer - 1][x])
        for y in reversed(range(layer + 1, h - layer)):
            result.append(grid[y][layer])
    return result
```

The loop peels one layer from the grid at a time, starting from the outer rim. The first inner loop goes right along the top of the current layer. The next three inner loops each run just once for every row or column and collect the right edge, bottom edge and left edge of the layer, sensitive to the parity of the edge with reversed(range) for the bottom and left passes. The layer count is capped by half the smaller of the two dimensions, so a single-cell grid or a one-row or one-column grid is handled as well. Each cell is therefore read exactly once by position.

